# PKTITLEqz7Wx3Vb

This paper is written in Markdown and built into Word with Pandoc. It was
made from the [trazo](https://github.com/joselznom/trazo) template;
the shared tools (preview, editors, checks, agent skills) come from
trazo. Requirements and installation are in trazo's README. In
the Claude desktop app with the trazo plugin, ask the agent to build the
Word files or open the preview.

In Terminal, run every command from this folder (`cd` into it first).

## First steps

```bash
scripts/trazo doctor   # check the tools; it says how to fix anything missing
scripts/trazo verify   # build everything and run every check
open output/manuscript.docx
```

Then open the live preview:

```bash
scripts/trazo build --prepare=inputs && scripts/trazo preview serve
```

and go to <http://127.0.0.1:8765>. Leave it running while you work; it
updates when a file changes. Stop it with Ctrl+C in Terminal.

## How you work

1. Write in `manuscript/main/` (one file per section) and
   `manuscript/supplementary/`. Use any text editor (VS Code, TextEdit in
   plain-text mode) or the preview's **Source** tab, or ask the agent.
2. Read the result in the preview. Select text to leave a comment for the
   agent; the **Diff** tab shows what changed since the last saved version.
3. Build the Word files (ask the agent, or `scripts/trazo build`):
   `output/manuscript.docx` and `output/supplementary.docx`.
4. Save a version when a piece of work is done:

   ```bash
   git add -A && git commit -m "Draft of the methods"
   ```

   Saved versions let you compare and undo, and the Diff tab compares
   against the last one.

Do not edit the Word file: it is rebuilt from the Markdown each time. Send
it to coauthors, and bring their changes back into the Markdown (the agent
can do it from their comments).

## Writing in Markdown

| You write | You get |
|---|---|
| `# Methods`, `## Participants` | Section and subsection headings |
| `*italic*`, `**bold**` | *italic*, **bold** |
| `[@smith2024]`, `[@smith2024; @lee2023]` | Citations, formatted by the journal style |
| `@smith2024 showed` | Author in the sentence |
| `[@fig:trend]`, `[@tbl:summary]` | "Figure 1", "Table 1", numbered automatically |
| `{{v:cohort.n}}` | A number computed from the data (see Numbers) |

A blank line starts a new paragraph. Section files are built in name
order, so keep the numbers (`02_methods.md`, `03_results.md`). Title,
authors and keywords are in `manuscript/metadata.yaml`.

## Document metadata

`kind` in `trazo.toml` is `article` or `report`; both use the same sections.

```yaml
author:
  - name: Your Name  # or {given: Your, family: Name}
    email: your.name@institution.org
    corresponding: true
    affiliations: [{ref: organisation}]
affiliations:
  - id: organisation
    name: Your Institution
keywords: [first term, second term]  # optional; shown after # Abstract
```

Plain-string authors still work; affiliations accept names, inline maps or
refs matching an id, and `equal-contributor: true` adds a shared-contribution note.
Reports start without keywords or correspondence; metadata edits rebuild the
title block in the next build, and updates leave this project-owned file alone.

Supplementary figures, tables and sections are numbered independently as
**Figure S1**, **Table S1**, **Section S1**, and so on, including their
references and exports. The manuscript cites them with the same labels
(`[@fig:name]` reads "Figure S1"), and the supplementary cites the
manuscript's the same way. To change the prefix, set `number_prefix` in the
document's `trazo.toml` entry; `title_prefix = "Supplementary"` gives
"Supplementary Figure S1" in captions and references.

## References

References live in `bibliography/references.bib` (BibTeX). Add them in one
of two ways:

- Ask the agent: "add the reference for DOI 10.1000/xyz" (the
  `add-reference` skill fetches it from the DOI, never from memory).
- From Zotero, with the Better BibTeX plugin: export the collection as
  Better BibTeX to `bibliography/references.bib`, with "Keep updated" on.

Cite with the key of the entry (`@smith2024`). `scripts/trazo build
--prepare=inputs && scripts/trazo citations` reports keys that are not in
the bibliography (the prepare renders the tables, which may cite too).

## Figures

- **Plots** are made by Python scripts in `figures/scripts/` from the data
  in `data/`, so they update when the data change. Each script needs a
  `[[build.steps]]` entry in `trazo.toml`, which `scripts/trazo build`
  and the preview's Word export run (the agent's `paper-figure-generation`
  skill knows how); `scripts/trazo build --prepare=inputs` rebuilds them.
- **Diagrams** (boxes and arrows) are edited in the preview at
  <http://127.0.0.1:8765/diagrams>, or by the agent.
- **Existing images**: put them in a folder such as `figures/images/`.

Insert a figure where it should appear, with a caption and a label. The
optional title is bold in the preview and plain in Word:

```markdown
![[Title of the figure.]{.caption-title} Legend.](figures/output/png/fig1_name.png){#fig:name width=100%}
```

and cite it as `[@fig:name]`. The preview's **Figures & Tables** view lists
every figure and table, with the places that cite them.

## Tables

A script computes the table's numbers from the data
(`tables/scripts/generate_tables.py`) and a spec
(`tables/specs/<label>.json`) sets the columns, headers, decimals and bold
cells. Insert the table with `!include tables/output/md/<label>.md` and
cite it as `[@tbl:label]`. In the preview, **Edit table** changes headers,
formats and layout with the mouse. Ask the agent to create a table (the
`paper-table-generation` skill); details are in `tables/README.md`.

## Numbers

Numbers in the text (sample size, means, percentages) are written as
`{{v:key}}` and computed by `scripts/build_values.py` into
`data/values.json`. When the data change, the next build (or
`scripts/trazo build --prepare=inputs`) updates every number in the text, so none are copied by hand. Ask the agent to add one
(the `paper-values` skill).

## Working with the agent

Open this folder in Claude Code (run `claude` in Terminal from here, or
choose the folder in the desktop app) or in Codex. The agent reads
`AGENTS.md` (the project rules) and the skills in `.claude/skills/` and
`.agents/skills/`. Ask in your own words; it picks the right skill. In
Claude Code you can also call one directly, for example `/critique`.

| Skill | Ask for example |
|---|---|
| `scientific-writing-style` | "Draft the Methods from these notes" |
| `critique` | "Review the Discussion as a peer reviewer would" |
| `comments` | "Answer my comments in the preview" |
| `add-reference` | "Add the reference for DOI 10.1000/xyz" |
| `citation-discipline` | "Check the citations" |
| `paper-figure-generation` | "Plot the score by visit from data/scores.csv" |
| `diagram-authoring` | "Draw a flow diagram of patient selection" |
| `paper-table-generation` | "Make a table of patient characteristics" |
| `paper-values` | "Compute the sample size from the data and use it in the text" |
| `reviewer-response` | "Draft the response to the reviewers' comments" |
| `audit-ai-writing` | "Check the Introduction for phrases that sound AI-written" |

The review loop: leave comments in the preview, ask the agent to answer
them, check its edits in the **Diff** tab, and save a version.
**Figures & Tables** opens the table and diagram editors. Editor links
return to the original document or gallery. **Export pending** on a diagram means its saved edits have not
reached the manuscript image; exporting or building Word updates it.
Diagrams marked as drafts keep the previous manuscript image.

## Journal format

The Word styles come from `templates/reference_universal.docx` and the
citation style from `bibliography/csl/ieee.csl`. For a journal, download
its style from the [Zotero Style Repository](https://www.zotero.org/styles)
into `bibliography/csl/`, add its Word template (if it has one) to
`templates/`, and set both in `trazo.toml`:

```toml
[paths]
csl = "bibliography/csl/journal.csl"
reference_doc = "templates/journal.docx"
```

`scripts/trazo build` and the preview read them there.

## Commands

```bash
scripts/trazo build --prepare=inputs  # values, tables and figures: run it before the next three
scripts/trazo preview serve           # live preview at http://127.0.0.1:8765 (Ctrl+C to stop)
scripts/trazo citations               # citation keys against the bibliography
scripts/trazo counts                  # main-text words, figures, tables, references
scripts/trazo build                   # every Word file
scripts/trazo build --doc manuscript  # output/manuscript.docx
scripts/trazo verify                  # rebuild everything and run every check
scripts/trazo doctor                  # check uv, Python, Pandoc and Chrome
scripts/trazo --help                  # every command
```

## Updates

Save your work first (`git add -A && git commit -m "..."`), then ask the
agent to update the paper (the plugin's `paper_update`). It brings the
template's changes (AGENTS.md, skills, scripts, the trazo version) of the
plugin's release into this project. A file you changed that the template
changed too gets a `<file>.new` beside it; ask the agent to merge it. Review
the changes in the Diff tab or with `git diff`.

## Problems

| Message | Fix |
|---|---|
| `trazo is not set up on this computer` | Ask the agent to set up trazo (the plugin's setup) |
| `Address already in use` (preview) | A preview is already running: use it, or stop it with Ctrl+C in its Terminal |
| `Chrome not found` | Ask the agent to set up trazo: it downloads a headless Chrome (or install Google Chrome from https://www.google.com/chrome/). Diagrams, the diagram editor, preview PNG/PDF exports and `scripts/trazo verify` need one |
| `values.json is stale` | `scripts/trazo build --prepare=inputs` |
| `Missing from bibliography/references.bib` | Add the reference, or fix the key in the text |

`scripts/trazo doctor` checks the installation. For anything else, paste the
error into the agent.

